<task id="201" project="customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>Implement H4b-BulkAppSettings handler: apply canonical BFF app-settings template in ONE batch (F20/F20a automation; kills progressive-fail-fast chain)</title>
    <phase>H-Prime — Absorb F19/F20 first-live findings into handler catalog</phase>
    <gate>blocked</gate>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Task tags include 'l2-controlplane','job-handler','auth','azure-deployment','config'; introduces a NEW handler primitive (bulk-app-settings-from-manifest) that ships ~80-160 app settings per BFF instance in ONE batch. .cs implementation. Any bug produces silent BFF fail-fast at boot (F20 pattern observed live 2026-08-24 with SIGABRT exit code 134 on `SpeAdmin:KeyVaultUri` missing). FR-36 canonical-manifest sibling. Blast radius = every BFF customer stamp.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Per project CLAUDE.md §Model tier defaults: Opus for Phase H canonical-manifest generator + high-blast-radius extensions. H4b introduces a second canonical manifest (app-settings, sibling to secret-catalog) — schema design must cover ~40 IOptions modules × ~2-4 settings with mixed sources (kv-ref / per-env-input / literal). Getting the schema right the first time avoids Model-2-dedicated churn + avoids the "add-one-setting-at-a-time" retrograde pattern that F20 demonstrated is untenable.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Depends on task 200 (H4-shared) landing first — H4b resolves KV refs to secrets H4-shared seeds. Depends on task 084 canonical-manifest pattern (which H4b's sibling app-settings manifest follows). Blocks H9 (BFF deploy) — H9 assumes app settings are populated. Serial: 084 → 200 → 201 → H9.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>036, 044, 084, 200</deps>
    <tags>l2-controlplane, job-handler, auth, azure-deployment, config</tags>
    <estimated-effort>8-12 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Implement the H4b-BulkAppSettings handler as an IProvisioningHandler in the L2 control-plane. H4b reads a NEW canonical app-settings manifest at `scripts/canonical-app-settings/manifest.yaml` (sibling to task 084's secret-catalog manifest), resolves each entry (KV-ref / per-env-input / literal), and applies ALL settings in ONE batch `az webapp config appsettings set --settings k1=v1 k2=v2 ...` call to trigger ONE App Service restart cycle. This kills the F20 progressive-fail-fast chain observed live 2026-08-24 where BFF SIGABRT'd at boot on `SpeAdmin:KeyVaultUri` missing, and after fix on `CosmosPersistence:Endpoint` missing, and would have continued for ~40 IOptions modules. Manifest source-of-truth: `docs/guides/SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md` § App Service settings.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Expert in IProvisioningHandler contract, App Service app-settings semantics (batch set → single restart; PATCH via `az webapp config appsettings set --settings k1=v1 k2=v2 ...`), the `@Microsoft.KeyVault(VaultName=X;SecretName=Y)` ref format, .NET IOptions `.ValidateOnStart()` fail-fast pattern (~40 modules in BFF), and Phase G canonical KV naming (§7.9). Follow ADRs strictly.</role>

  <goal>Files exist: (a) `scripts/canonical-app-settings/manifest.yaml` enumerating every BFF app setting with source metadata; (b) `scripts/canonical-app-settings/Invoke-AppSettingsCatalogGenerator.ps1` generator (fans out to tokens.md + Bicep app-settings block + IOptions binding audit report); (c) `src/server/services/Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane/Handlers/BulkAppSettings/H4bBulkAppSettingsHandler.cs` implementing IProvisioningHandler; registers in L2 DI; Handler: (i) reads manifest; (ii) resolves each entry (kv-ref → `@Microsoft.KeyVault(...)` string / per-env-input → interStepState lookup / literal → verbatim); (iii) computes DIFF against current App Service app settings (read via `az webapp config appsettings list`); (iv) if diff non-empty, PATCHes ALL differing settings in ONE batch call → ONE restart cycle; (v) polls `/healthz` with warm-up backoff (30s / 60s / 90s / 120s / 180s = 8min budget); (vi) if `/healthz` never green, quarantine with fail-fast diagnostic (which IOptions module raised); (vii) idempotency key `appsettings-{env}-{manifestVer}`.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>
Per lessons-learned-model1-prod-standup-2026-08-22.md finding F20/F20a + Automation Gaps roadmap item 13 in `.claude/skills/provision-environment/SKILL.md`: BFF container exits with code 134 (SIGABRT) at startup with `Unhandled exception. System.InvalidOperationException: SpeAdmin:KeyVaultUri (or KeyVaultUri) configuration is required for SpeAdminModule.` This is one of ~40 `.ValidateOnStart()` chains — each missing config surfaces as fail-fast in sequence. Session 2 observed the pattern after fixing F20 (added `SpeAdmin__KeyVaultUri`), next fail-fast was `CosmosPersistence:Endpoint` missing (F20a), and chose NOT to serially chase 40-module chain.

The RIGHT fix is to set ALL required app settings in ONE batch call. That's what H4b does. It reads a manifest that enumerates every BFF app setting the ~40 IOptions modules require (source-of-truth: `docs/guides/SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md` § App Service settings), resolves each entry against its source (KV ref / per-env input / literal), and applies them all at once.

CLUSTER FINDING (from SESSION 2 handoff): F17 + F19 + F20 share ONE root cause — Bicep provisions Model 1 Prod infra but does NOT seed BFF runtime state. H4-shared (task 200) + H4b (this task) + H9 (BFF deploy) together close this gap. Sequencing: H4-shared → H4b → H9 → BFF starts with all config in place (kills F20 progressive fail-fast).

**Per-env inputs** (settings that vary per customer — cannot be extracted from source services):
- `TenantId` — the customer's Azure AD tenant GUID (from H0 preflight)
- `AzureAd:ClientId` — the multitenant BFF app's client ID (from H3 Entra app-reg output)
- `SharePointEmbedded:ContainerTypeId` — SPE container type (from H8 output)
- `WebhookSigningKey` — SPE webhook signing key (from H8 output)
- `WebhookClientState` — SPE webhook client state (from H8 output)
- `EmailProcessing:WebhookSigningKey` — Email webhook signing key (from H14 output)
- (others per full BFF IOptions inventory — task-execute Step 1 discovery)

These come from prior-handler `interStepState` (Cosmos-persisted; ADR-004 shape). H4b resolves them by key lookup from run state.

**KV-refs** (settings that resolve to KV secrets seeded by H4 per-tenant or H4-shared):
- 6 shared-KV refs seeded by H4-shared (task 200): `AzureOpenAI:ApiKey`, `AiSearch:AdminKey`, `DocumentIntelligence:ApiKey`, `ServiceBus:ConnectionString`, `Storage:ConnectionString`, `Redis:ConnectionString`
- Per-tenant refs seeded by H4 (task 047): `Dataverse:ClientSecret`, `BFF-API:ClientSecret`, others
- Refs use format: `@Microsoft.KeyVault(VaultName=sprk-{env}-kv;SecretName=AiSearch--AdminKey)`
    </background>
