  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Rigor + load">Emit RIGOR LEVEL (FULL). Load knowledge files. Load `docs/guides/SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md` § App Service settings.</step>
    <step order="1" name="BFF IOptions inventory">Enumerate every `AddOptions&lt;T&gt;().Bind(...).ValidateOnStart()` chain in `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/DI/*Module.cs` files (~40 modules). For each: (a) module name, (b) IOptions type, (c) config section key (e.g., `SpeAdmin`, `CosmosPersistence`), (d) fields required by validator. Produce inventory table. This is the manifest source.</step>
    <step order="2" name="Manifest schema">Design `scripts/canonical-app-settings/manifest.yaml` schema (mirror task 084 shape): entries have `key`, `source`, `kv-secret-name?`, `per-env-key?`, `literal-value?`, `iOptionsModule`, `required` (default true).</step>
    <step order="3" name="Write manifest">Author `scripts/canonical-app-settings/manifest.yaml` with every entry from the BFF IOptions inventory. Categorize each: kv-ref (from H4-shared or H4-per-tenant seeded secrets) / per-env-input (from prior-handler interStepState) / literal (constant across all envs). Cross-check against `docs/guides/SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md`.</step>
    <step order="4" name="Generator">Author `scripts/canonical-app-settings/Invoke-AppSettingsCatalogGenerator.ps1` — fans out to: (a) `tokens.md` (documentation), (b) Bicep app-settings block snippet (paste-into `app-service.bicep`), (c) IOptions binding audit report (diff manifest keys vs BFF `.ValidateOnStart()` requirements → flag orphans + gaps). Deterministic ordering.</step>
    <step order="5" name="IAppSettingsManifestReader">Author `IAppSettingsManifestReader.cs` + `YamlAppSettingsManifestReader.cs` — parses manifest.yaml into `IReadOnlyList&lt;AppSettingEntry&gt;`. BINDING guard enforced at read-time.</step>
    <step order="6" name="IAppSettingsResolver">Author `IAppSettingsResolver.cs` + `AppSettingsResolver.cs` — resolves each entry: kv-ref → `@Microsoft.KeyVault(...)` string composition; per-env-input → Cosmos interStepState lookup; literal → verbatim value. Fails startup if a required per-env-input is missing from interStepState.</step>
    <step order="7" name="IAppSettingsWriter">Author `IAppSettingsWriter.cs` + `AzCliAppSettingsWriter.cs` — wraps `az webapp config appsettings set --settings k1=v1 k2=v2 ...` batch call. Sanitizes values (shell-escapes; the `@Microsoft.KeyVault(...)` format is safe — no shell metacharacters).</step>
    <step order="8" name="IHealthzProbe">Author `IHealthzProbe.cs` + `HttpHealthzProbe.cs` — polls `https://{appServiceName}.azurewebsites.net/healthz` with backoff (30s / 60s / 90s / 120s / 180s = 8-min budget). Success = HTTP 200. On failure: attempts to fetch container logs via Kudu API `/api/logs/docker` + parses for `Unhandled exception. System.InvalidOperationException:` line → extracts module name for diagnostic.</step>
    <step order="9" name="H4bBulkAppSettingsHandler">Author main handler: inject IAppSettingsManifestReader, IAppSettingsResolver, IAppSettingsWriter, IHealthzProbe, IIdempotencyService, ICosmosRunStore. HandleAsync: (a) read manifest; (b) resolve all entries against interStepState (fail early on missing per-env-inputs); (c) fetch current app settings via `az webapp config appsettings list`; (d) compute diff (missing + mismatched); (e) NO-OP if diff empty; else batch-write via IAppSettingsWriter; (f) poll /healthz via IHealthzProbe; (g) on green, mark Succeeded + persist manifestVer to Cosmos; (h) on red, Quarantine with parsed fail-fast diagnostic.</step>
    <step order="10" name="Program.cs registration">Register H4bBulkAppSettingsHandler + supporting services in L2 Program.cs DI. Wire into HandlerIds catalog (task 103).</step>
    <step order="11" name="Unit tests">Author unit tests: (a) full-manifest happy path (mock resolver returns all values, writer succeeds, healthz returns 200); (b) diff-empty NO-OP (current == desired → 0 writes); (c) partial-diff batch-write (only diffing settings included in batch); (d) required-per-env-input-missing quarantine (interStepState doesn't have `TenantId` → QuarantineRequired BEFORE writes); (e) /healthz-never-green quarantine (mock probe returns 500 for full backoff window → QuarantineRequired with parsed diagnostic); (f) BINDING guard (manifest tampered with `Dataverse-ClientSecret` as literal → manifest read fails); (g) idempotency (two runs same manifestVer → second is short-circuit no-op); (h) diff preserves operator overrides (setting present on App Service but not in manifest → NOT touched); (i) IOptions binding audit report (verify generator output flags manifest-vs-BFF diffs).</step>
    <step order="12" name="Live-fire test bed prep">Document how to run against Model 1 Prod as first live-fire test: L2 UAMI needs `Website Contributor` role on `sprksharedprod-api`; L2 must be reachable from operator machine via L2 API. Expected first-run outcome: H4b writes ~80-160 app settings in one batch, App Service restarts once, /healthz returns 200 within warm-up window. If /healthz stays red, manifest needs an entry — extend + re-run (idempotent).</step>
    <step order="13" name="Build + test">Run `dotnet build src/server/services/Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane/` + `dotnet test`. Verify green + zero analyzer warnings.</step>
    <step order="14" name="Handler-catalog update">Update handler catalog in `.claude/skills/provision-environment/SKILL.md` Step 3 handler enumeration to list H4b alongside H4 + H4-shared (main-session-only per root CLAUDE.md §3 sub-agent write boundary).</step>
    <step order="15" name="Index update">Update TASK-INDEX.md: task 201 → ✅.</step>
    <step order="16" name="Deviations">Document any deviation in projects/customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1/notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If task 200 (H4-shared) has NOT landed, STOP and escalate — H4b's KV-ref entries resolve to secrets H4-shared seeds. Running H4b without H4-shared produces `KeyVaultReferenceCouldNotResolve` at App Service runtime.</trigger>
    <trigger>If BFF IOptions inventory (Step 1) reveals &gt;60 modules (significantly more than the ~40 expected), STOP and escalate — this signals ADR-010 DI-minimalism drift + should trigger a decomposition project BEFORE H4b lands. Manifest scope explodes proportionally.</trigger>
    <trigger>If required per-env-input is not yet emitted by any upstream handler (e.g., `WebhookSigningKey` missing from H8's interStepState), STOP and escalate — H4b cannot fabricate the value; the upstream handler must be extended first.</trigger>
    <trigger>If /healthz-never-green diagnostic parses a fail-fast module name NOT present in the manifest, STOP and add manifest entry → re-run. If the parse also names a module NOT present in the BFF IOptions inventory (Step 1), the inventory is stale — re-run Step 1 first.</trigger>
    <trigger>If /healthz stays green but a downstream handler (H10, H11, H12a) fails on config the manifest missed, treat as manifest completeness gap and extend H4b's manifest. `.ValidateOnStart()` catches boot-time; runtime code paths that read `IConfiguration` lazily are H4b's blind spot (small — most BFF code binds IOptions).</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>Closest neighbors: (a) H4 per-tenant handler (task 047) — writes to per-tenant KV, does NOT set App Service app settings. (b) `az webapp config appsettings set` ad-hoc invocation — session 2 used this manually to set ONE setting (`SpeAdmin__KeyVaultUri`), revealing F20a next fail-fast. (c) Bicep `app-service.bicep` `appSettings` array — sets initial app settings at deploy time, but stale across upgrades + can't resolve per-env inputs from run state. Grep for `AppSettingsManifest` returns none.</existing>
    <extension>No — H4 per-tenant CANNOT be extended to also do app-settings because (a) different target (KV secrets vs App Service app settings), (b) different idempotency (H4 rotation-safe; H4b diff-and-restart-once), (c) different failure mode (H4 write failure = quarantine; H4b write failure = restart + /healthz probe → parse container logs). Bicep `app-service.bicep`'s `appSettings` array CANNOT be extended to resolve per-env inputs from Cosmos run state — Bicep is static; app settings depend on prior-handler outputs. Two sibling handlers (H4-shared + H4b) plus H4-per-tenant keep each contract tight.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Without H4b as a handler: (1) Every new BFF customer stamp requires MANUAL enumeration of ~40 IOptions modules → ~80-160 app settings → serial `az webapp config appsettings set` calls, each triggering an App Service restart = ~40 restarts × ~3 min each = 2-hour manual runbook per customer (this session's F20 progressive discovery would have taken this long if pursued). (2) Every BFF module addition (new IOptions binding) silently breaks new customer stand-ups until an operator manually adds the app setting — no drift detection today. (3) F20's fail-fast pattern (SIGABRT exit 134) will recur on EVERY new Model 1 shared-tier deploy AND on EVERY Model 2 Dedicated deploy — this is not a Model-1-specific bug; it's the fresh-deploy-without-bulk-config bug. Concrete observed failure mode: `Unhandled exception. System.InvalidOperationException: SpeAdmin:KeyVaultUri (or KeyVaultUri) configuration is required for SpeAdminModule.` at BFF container startup, followed by SIGABRT (exit 134), followed by App Service marking container Failed to start. Repeat for each of ~40 modules.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <tools>
    <tool name="dotnet">Build and test .NET projects</tool>
    <tool name="terminal">Run shell + az CLI for batch app-settings write + /healthz poll + Kudu docker-logs fetch</tool>
    <tool name="pwsh">Author Invoke-AppSettingsCatalogGenerator.ps1</tool>
  </tools>

