  <notes>Reference: lessons-learned-model1-prod-standup-2026-08-22.md finding F20/F20a + Automation Gaps roadmap item 13 + spec.md FR-36 canonical-manifest pattern + NFR-05 fail-fast + design.md §4.1 H4 (sibling handler shape). Companion task: 200 (H4-shared). This session (2026-08-24) manually validated the F20 fail-fast pattern against Model 1 Prod — set `SpeAdmin__KeyVaultUri`, revealed F20a on `CosmosPersistence:Endpoint`, chose NOT to serially chase ~40-module chain. That serial chase is precisely what this handler eliminates. Live-fire test bed: Model 1 Prod (`sprksharedprod-api` App Service).

## Design notes (2026-08-24, SESSION 3 — H4b/H4-shared design)

**Why a NEW canonical manifest (vs. extending task 084's secret-catalog manifest)**:
- Task 084's manifest is scoped to KV secrets (canonical name, purpose, consumers, rotation cadence, never-delete). Adding app-settings would require overloading the schema OR adding a separate `app-settings:` top-level list. Cleaner: sibling manifest at `scripts/canonical-app-settings/manifest.yaml` mirroring the same shape.
- The two manifests are RELATED: app-settings manifest entries with `source: kv-ref, kv-secret-name: X` implicitly cross-reference secret-catalog manifest entries with canonical name X. Generator's IOptions audit report should ALSO flag KV-ref app-settings that name a secret NOT in the secret-catalog manifest (cross-manifest drift detection).

**Why /healthz probe with 8-min backoff (not shorter)**:
- BFF cold-start observed 2026-08-24: ~2-4 min from restart to /healthz-green (assuming no fail-fast). Warm-up wants generous budget to distinguish "still starting" from "fail-fast permanent". 8-min budget covers 99th-percentile cold-start + gives fail-fast diagnostic clear signal (if not green by 8min, it's not going to be).
- The backoff schedule (30s / 60s / 90s / 120s / 180s) is exponential-ish; each probe is a HEAD request (cheap). 5 probes across 8 min.

**Why parse container docker-logs on failure**:
- The `.ValidateOnStart()` fail-fast exception is UI-invisible unless operator fetches Kudu `/api/logs/docker`. Session 2 did this manually. H4b's IHealthzProbe automates it — extracts the `Unhandled exception. System.InvalidOperationException:` line, parses the message for the module name, reports it. This turns a "BFF broke" quarantine into a "H4b manifest missing SpeAdmin__KeyVaultUri" quarantine — actionable diagnostic.

**Cross-manifest sequencing / DAG assertion**:
- H4-per-tenant (task 047): seeds per-tenant KV
- H4-shared (task 200): seeds shared KV from source Azure services
- H4b (task 201): applies bulk app-settings referencing KV secrets from both
- H9 (task 052): deploys BFF code
- Correct order: H4 || H4-shared → H4b → H9 → BFF starts configured

If any handler runs out of order, downstream fails deterministically (H4b Quarantine on missing KV secret; H9 boot fail-fast). Order-enforcement lives in the L2 state machine DAG (task 058 state reconciler).

**Model 2 Dedicated extensibility**: manifest schema field `source: kv-ref | per-env-input | literal` naturally extends. For M2, per-env-inputs may become per-customer-inputs; the resolver's Cosmos interStepState lookup already scopes by run-id / customer-id.
  </notes>

